grommunio-archive for Debian
============================

grommunio Archive is piler (mailpiler.org) with grommunio's changes. Mail is
copied to it by the MTA; it is stored encrypted and compressed, indexed with
Sphinx and searched through a PHP web interface.

The package installs the software but does not configure it. Run, as root,
after the installation:

    grommunio-archive-setup [-f FQDN] [-d DOMAIN] [-H dbhost] [-U dbuser] [-D dbname] [-P] [-F]

It needs a running MariaDB/MySQL server on this host (or an existing empty
database and user with -H/-U/-D and ARCHIVE_MYSQL_PASS in the environment) and
does the following:

  * creates the database "groarchive" and the user "groarchive" and loads the
    schema with the archive users admin@local and auditor@local (their
    generated passwords are shown once at the end, and written to
    /var/log/grommunio-archive/setup.log, mode 0600: delete that file);
  * writes /etc/grommunio-archive/grommunio-archive.conf (the daemons),
    config-site.php (the web interface) and grommunio-archive.key (56 random
    bytes; back it up: without it encrypted messages cannot be read);
  * creates /etc/sphinx/sphinx.conf from the template and builds the search
    index;
  * enables and starts grommunio-archive-searchd, grommunio-archive-smtp and
    grommunio-archive;
  * wires Postfix (unless -P is given): always_bcc=archive@FQDN, a transport
    entry that sends that address to 127.0.0.1:2693, and a pcre map that adds
    an X-Envelope-To header. It needs the packages postfix-pcre and (for the
    map type) postfix-lmdb or the default map type of your Postfix.
    "grommunio-archive-setup -r" removes this wiring again.

The setup refuses to overwrite an existing configuration without -F; the
database and its users are kept in that case.

Where things are
----------------
  web interface   https://FQDN/archive/  (nginx snippets from this package are
                  in /usr/share/grommunio-common/nginx; PHP-FPM pool
                  grommunio-archive, socket /run/php/php-grommunio-archive-fpm.sock;
                  reload nginx and PHP-FPM after the installation)
  archive SMTP    127.0.0.1:2693 (piler-smtp; set listen_addr in
                  grommunio-archive.conf to change it, and use
                  smtp.acl from /usr/share/grommunio-archive/dist to restrict)
  search daemon   127.0.0.1:9312 and 9306 (Sphinx)
  messages        /var/lib/grommunio-archive/store  (the archive itself)
  configuration   /etc/grommunio-archive, /etc/sphinx/sphinx.conf
  templates       /usr/share/grommunio-archive/dist
  cron jobs       /etc/cron.d/grommunio-archive (index updates, purge,
                  statistics; they need a cron daemon)
  helper scripts  /usr/libexec/grommunio-archive

Text extraction
---------------
The daemon extracts text from attachments with pdftotext (poppler-utils),
catdoc (Word), catppt and xls2csv (PowerPoint and Excel, both from catdoc), unrtf
(RTF) and tnef (winmail.dat). The alternative ppthtml (package xlhtml) is not
available in Debian and is not needed.

Security notes
--------------
The tools pilerget, pileraget, pilerimport, pilerexport, pilerstats, pilertest
and reindex are setuid/setgid groarchive. They are installed 0755 and changed
with dpkg-statoverride in the postinst; "dpkg-statoverride --list | grep piler"
shows the entries, which are removed when the package is purged.

Removal
-------
"apt remove" keeps everything. "apt purge" removes the package's files and
the statoverrides, but keeps the archive (/var/lib/grommunio-archive), the
configuration in /etc/grommunio-archive, the database and the encryption key:
they are your data. Remove them by hand when the archive is really to go.

Backup: the database "groarchive", /var/lib/grommunio-archive/store,
/etc/grommunio-archive (especially grommunio-archive.key).

 -- Louis van Belle <louis@van-belle.nl>, Thu, 24 Sep 2026
